WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:09.380
This is the Korea Daily Brief from the CSIS Korea Chair, read by an AI voice. It's Tuesday, October 27.

2
00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:20.154
Three things today: South Korea's economy surges past expectations, North Korea moves artillery to the western border, and Switzerland is heading to Pyongyang.

3
00:00:20.154 --> 00:00:27.947
On that first story: the numbers are strong, but the Bank of Korea is already warning about what could unwind them.

4
00:00:27.947 --> 00:00:38.450
On Wednesday, Swiss Foreign Minister Ignazio Cassis begins a two-day visit to North Korea, the highest-level Western diplomatic trip to Pyongyang in years.

5
00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:53.264
First, South Korea's economy. The Bank of Korea reported Tuesday that real GDP grew 1.0 percent in the third quarter, quarter-on-quarter. That's triple the Bank of Korea's own forecast of 0.3 percent.

6
00:00:53.264 --> 00:01:03.270
The engine is semiconductors, AI-driven chip exports have surged, and some analysts now see annual growth reaching 4 percent. That's the good news.

7
00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:13.481
The same day, the Bank of Korea flagged a significant downside risk: chip exports could fall by up to 100 billion dollars if global AI spending slows.

8
00:01:13.481 --> 00:01:20.900
So the strength and the vulnerability come from exactly the same place. Consumer confidence is also slipping.

9
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:34.991
The Bank of Korea's composite consumer sentiment index fell to 105.4 in October, down 1.2 points from September. The bank cited prolonged Middle East tensions and a stock market correction as the main drags.

10
00:01:34.991 --> 00:01:41.253
On the political side, Samsung Electronics Chairman Lee Jae-yong is getting attention today.

11
00:01:41.253 --> 00:01:56.637
The Korea Herald profiles his four-year tenure steering the company from a memory downturn into the current AI chip boom, with Samsung's high-bandwidth memory and foundry business now central to the AI infrastructure buildout.

12
00:01:56.637 --> 00:02:03.240
The headline number to carry: GDP up 1.0 percent in the third quarter, against a forecast of 0.3.

13
00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.742
Second, North Korea's western border.

14
00:02:06.742 --> 00:02:21.238
Photography from the Aegibong observatory in Gimpo, South Korea, captured at least three North Korean 122-millimeter self-propelled howitzers deployed along the Jogang River coastal border area of Kaepung County in Hwanghae Province.

15
00:02:21.238 --> 00:02:28.579
N.K. News published the images. The guns are farther south than their usual positions, visible from South Korean soil.

16
00:02:28.579 --> 00:02:37.289
South Korea's Joint Chiefs of Staff assessed these as legacy systems, not the new artillery Pyongyang recently threatened to forward-deploy.

17
00:02:37.289 --> 00:02:49.856
The Joint Chiefs also noted the deployment was, in their words, "in plain view." At the National Assembly on the same day, Defense Minister Kang Shin-chul made a remark that sparked its own controversy.

18
00:02:49.856 --> 00:03:03.419
Kang asked, "Can we really trust the U.S. military?" The Defense Ministry moved quickly to clarify: the comment was made to push back on lawmakers skeptical of wartime operational control transfer, not to question U.S.

19
00:03:03.419 --> 00:03:16.049
reliability as an ally. Still, the remark landed in public on the same day North Korean guns appeared on the border. Unification Minister Chung Dong-young offered his own read of North Korean intentions.

20
00:03:16.049 --> 00:03:28.678
Chung told the National Assembly that Pyongyang's hostile statements reflect its plan to complete demarcating the inter-Korean border, what Seoul calls "borderization", on schedule. South Korean and U.S.

21
00:03:28.678 --> 00:03:40.001
defense ministers are also set to meet in the Philippines on the sidelines of the ASEAN Defense Ministers' Meeting, where wartime operational control transfer is expected to come up.

22
00:03:40.001 --> 00:03:50.080
The bottom line: artillery forward on the western border, an alliance remark requiring a ministry walkback, and an inter-Korean border demarcation still underway.

23
00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:59.724
Third, Switzerland heads to Pyongyang. Swiss Foreign Minister Ignazio Cassis will travel to North Korea on October 29 for a two-day visit.

24
00:03:59.724 --> 00:04:07.679
He'll attend the official opening of the first dedicated Swiss political office in Pyongyang and meet with North Korean officials.

25
00:04:07.679 --> 00:04:20.406
Switzerland has long served as a protecting power for the United States in North Korea, making it one of the few countries with sustained official access to Pyongyang. The Swiss government confirmed the trip.

26
00:04:20.406 --> 00:04:27.320
It's the highest-level Western diplomatic visit to North Korea in years. That visit begins Wednesday, October 29.

27
00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:34.830
Now to Pyongyang. Kim Jong Un has not made a public appearance today, that's two days since his last.

28
00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:42.389
State media led with a reply message Kim sent to Vietnamese President To Lam, pledging to strengthen bilateral relations.

29
00:04:42.389 --> 00:04:51.510
KCNA also reported the opening of the 18th Pyongyang Autumn International Trade Fair, which attracted foreign participants despite U.N. sanctions.

30
00:04:51.510 --> 00:05:03.692
And North Korean officials laid wreaths at the Chinese People's Volunteers Friendship Tower, continuing commemorations tied to the Korean War anniversary and Kim's recent visit to Dandong, China.

31
00:05:03.692 --> 00:05:15.250
The bottom line: KCNA's lineup reinforces the diplomatic and symbolic framing around Kim's Dandong trip, while the trade fair signals North Korea's continued outreach despite sanctions.

32
00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:28.461
The rest of the news, quickly. The Supreme Court issued an additional arrest warrant for former first lady Kim Gun-hee, extending her detention. The Democratic Party called it a natural result.

33
00:05:28.461 --> 00:05:42.090
Separately, the Democratic Party resolved Tuesday to pursue a national audit of Supreme Court Chief Justice Cho Hui-dae over alleged interference in judicial affairs. The party is targeting a plenary vote on November 5.

34
00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:53.727
At the National Assembly Foreign Affairs and Unification Committee, Foreign Minister Cho Hyun testified Tuesday that the United States has asked Seoul not to get ahead on North Korea-U.S.

35
00:05:53.727 --> 00:06:07.294
dialogue, both sides should pace their engagement together. Cho also said he'll raise the issue of U.S. visa denials for a South Korean lawmaker directly with Secretary of State Marco Rubio. On the visa point: the U.S.

36
00:06:07.294 --> 00:06:20.674
visitor visa refusal rate for South Koreans has reached nearly 25 percent, more than four times another country's rate, the highest on record. A fire broke out Tuesday at Guryong Village in Seoul's Gangnam district.

37
00:06:20.674 --> 00:06:29.698
President Lee Jae-myung ordered all available resources deployed. Thirty-nine residents were evacuated, and no casualties were reported. The U.S.

38
00:06:29.698 --> 00:06:41.336
Treasury removed two Chinese animation firms, Moxing Cartoon and Shanghai Hongman, along with a Chinese national named Lu Hezheng from its North Korea sanctions list, without explanation.

39
00:06:41.336 --> 00:06:53.471
Six North Koreans were killed when a train struck their vehicle in Russia's Jewish Autonomous Region. Russian authorities opened a criminal investigation. A seventh passenger survived. Daily N.K.

40
00:06:53.471 --> 00:07:06.913
reports that North Korea is conducting a final screening of unmarried women in their 20s for Chinese factory jobs, with deployment possible by December. Recruitment closed in South Pyongan Province in late September.

41
00:07:06.913 --> 00:07:07.660
A Daily N.K.

42
00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:21.040
report also finds that a prolonged smuggling crackdown has wiped out hard-currency streams for state agencies, the military, and the Workers' Party, pushing units into low-paying Chinese contract manufacturing work.

43
00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:30.500
The CSIS Korea Chair published a podcast with Korea Risk Group analyst Jeongmin Kim on what Kim Jong Un's Dandong bridge crossing signals for diplomacy.

44
00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:39.150
A CSIS analysis also argues that recent North Korean missile activity may be a prelude to diplomatic summitry rather than pure provocation.

45
00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:47.800
The Gallup Korea poll, taken October 13 to 15, shows presidential approval at 39 percent, up from the prior survey.

46
00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:55.640
The Democratic Party stands at 36 percent, the People Power Party at 27 percent, and independents at 29 percent.

47
00:07:55.640 --> 00:08:09.780
Also ahead: South Korea's government consumer promotion campaign, the Korea Grand Festival, opens October 29 and runs through November 15, offering discounts of up to 50 percent across a range of goods.

48
00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:24.274
Beyond the region. South Korea hosted the first International Atomic Energy Agency Nuclear Energy Ministerial Conference ever held in the country, opening Tuesday at Incheon Songdo with 60 nations represented.

49
00:08:24.274 --> 00:08:31.906
Seoul used the occasion to promote its small modular reactor agenda and position Korea as a nuclear technology exporter.

50
00:08:31.906 --> 00:08:42.910
Separately, India is actively pursuing shipbuilding cooperation with South Korea, seeking Korean expertise and technology transfer for its own maritime industrial ambitions.

51
00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:59.923
Finally, the markets. Seoul's main stock index, the KOSPI, closed up about 3 percent. The won traded at about 1,327 to the dollar, down slightly. Brent crude fell about 5 percent to just over 110 dollars a barrel.

52
00:08:59.923 --> 00:09:12.840
The Bank of Korea's policy rate stands at 2.50 percent. That's the Korea Daily Brief for Tuesday, October 27. The full text, with a link to every source, is in today's email.
